# AI and LLM Rules

## Provider Independence

LLM usage must go through an application-level interface.

Do not couple core logic directly to a specific provider.

Prefer a contract similar to:

```ts
interface LlmClient {
  generate<T>(input: LlmRequest): Promise<T>
}
```

The exact interface may evolve with implementation needs.

## Structured Output

Prefer structured outputs over free-form text when the result feeds application logic.

Validate all LLM-generated structured data with Zod.

Never trust an LLM response merely because it parsed successfully.

## Prompt Design

Prompts should:

- clearly define the task
- define expected output
- include relevant project context
- avoid unnecessary verbosity
- separate instructions from user-provided content

Keep prompts versionable and testable where practical.

## Reliability

For recoverable LLM failures:

- use bounded retries
- validate every attempt
- avoid infinite retry loops
- preserve useful error information

Do not hide provider failures behind generic success responses.

## Security

Treat user-provided project descriptions and generated content as untrusted data.

Consider prompt injection when external content is included in LLM input.

Never expose:

- API keys
- secrets
- internal credentials
- private environment variables

to generated output.

## Responsibility Boundary

LLMs may help with:

- extracting structured requirements
- suggesting project context
- generating drafts

LLMs must not become the only source of truth for domain validation.
